p: .
t. 
|  Type-theoretically, modeling some parts of speech as
|  functions captures the
|  interplay of stasis and dynamics that typifies
.  cognitive staging


--- Sentence/switch
id: 361
r#  62323  1169  24


--- Sentence//end
id: 361
r#  62433  1171  32
p: .
t. 
|   Noun-like conceptual
|  aggregates have a kind of holistic unity that becomes
.  destabilized by added modifiers


--- Sentence/switch
id: 362
r#  62434  1171  33


--- Sentence//end
id: 362
r#  62636  1175  25
p: .
t. 
|   Tin box, say,
|  has a certain stasis as idea, semi-image, or
|  precursor to action; a stasis disrupted by red, but
|  then via further processing the staged image settles
.  back into a stable unity


--- Sentence/switch
id: 363
r#  62637  1175  26


--- Sentence//end
id: 363
r#  62933  1180  40
p: .
t. 
|   The formal construct
|  of adjectives mapping nouns to nouns captures the
|  surface-level manifestation of a churning cognitive
|  underground, \clrmAlt{11}{67}where intellectual labor is expended in
|  forming, altering (by assimilating new detail), and
.  re-establishing static precursor-images


--- Sentence/switch
id: 364
r#  62934  1180  41


--- Sentence//end
id: 364
r#  63236  1186  15
p: .
t. 
|   Similarly, propositions
|  have a kind of situational stasis  -  representations of
|  states of affairs more than
|  discrete objects, but still fixpoints within
|  our construal of facts and affordances surrounding us  - 
|  which gets destabilized when subordinate clauses become nested into
.  superordinates

--- Sentence/end 
id: 364
r#  63237  1186  16

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 66
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 67
r#  63239  1188  1


--- Sentence/start
id: 365
r#  63239  1188  1

--- Sentence//end
id: 365
r#  63362  1189  37
p: .
t. 
|  (Syntactic-) Categorial type theories are, of course, just a few among many protocols
.  deployed to model sentence structure


--- Sentence/switch
id: 366
r#  63363  1189  38


--- Sentence//end
id: 366
r#  63420  1190  19
p: .
t. 
|   Phrase-structure grammars emphasize
.  nested hierarchies


--- Sentence/switch
id: 367
r#  63421  1190  20


--- Sentence//end
id: 367
r#  63692  1194  52
p: .
t. 
|   Dependency grammars
|  focus more on inter-word relations (often among adjacent words, but crucial
|  connections can exist between distant ones, such as pronoun/antecedent,
|  or subject to verb when a clause intervenes)
.  [/Kahane/], [/TimothyOsborne/], [/GeroldSchneider/]


--- Sentence/switch
id: 368
r#  63693  1194  53


--- Sentence//end
id: 368
r#  64017  1200  45
p: .
t. 
|  If our framework is instead (or in addition) more
|  cognitive, we can emphasize the transformative effects
|  of any single word on a conceptual synthesis established
|  via preceding words, in a logical ordering that
|  deviates from the sequence of enunciation, though grammar
.  determines how the latter maps to the former

--- Sentence/end 
id: 368
r#  64018  1200  46

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 67
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 68
r#  64022  1202  1


--- Sentence/start
id: 369
r#  64022  1202  1

--- Sentence//end
id: 369
r#  64255  1205  59
p: .
t. 
|  To examine further, imagine a simple programming language with
|  just a few components: function symbols, string (character-sequence)
|  and numeric literals, and variable
.  symbols that must be assigned a value before they are used


--- Sentence/switch
id: 370
r#  64256  1205  60


--- Sentence//end
id: 370
r#  64387  1207  38
p: .
t. 
|  The most common structure in such a language would be expressions consisting of a function
.  symbol and one or more arguments


--- Sentence/switch
id: 371
r#  64388  1207  39


--- Sentence//end
id: 371
r#  64534  1210  8
p: .
t. 
|   The interpreter
|  that would execute programs written in the language is essentially
|  a state machine, reading a sequence of symbols
.  in turn


--- Sentence/switch
id: 372
r#  64535  1210  9


--- Sentence//end
id: 372
r#  64602  1214  3
p: .
t. 
.   Assume the following basic operations: 1


--- Sentence/switch
id: 373
r#  64603  1214  4


--- Sentence//end
id: 373
r#  64723  1215  43
p: .
t. 
|   If the current symbol is a numeric literal, convert it to an actual number
.  (e.g., 16 becomes hexadecimal )


--- Sentence/switch
id: 374
r#  64724  1215  44


--- Sentence//end
id: 374
r#  64818  1217  48
p: .
t. 
|   Convert
|  string literals to arrays of glyph
.  codes (e.g., in the Unicode  format)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 375
r#  64819  1217  49


--- Sentence//end
id: 375
r#  64864  1218  25
p: .
t. 
|   Place the current
.  value on a program stack


--- Sentence/switch
id: 376
r#  64867  1220  1


--- Sentence//end
id: 376
r#  64869  1220  3
p: .
t. 
.  2


--- Sentence/switch
id: 377
r#  64870  1220  4


--- Sentence//end
id: 377
r#  64948  1221  30
p: .
t. 
|   If the current symbol is a variable, retrieve
.  its value from a global index


--- Sentence/switch
id: 378
r#  64949  1221  31


--- Sentence//end
id: 378
r#  65149  1224  77
p: .
t. 
|   Because every
|  variable is initialized before use, we can assume that
|  any value is ultimately obtained from source code
.  as in item , or read from an external source (like a local file)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 379
r#  65150  1224  78


--- Sentence//end
id: 379
r#  65200  1225  50
p: .
t. 
.  Again, place the current value on a program stack


--- Sentence/switch
id: 380
r#  65203  1227  1


--- Sentence//end
id: 380
r#  65205  1227  3
p: .
t. 
.  3


--- Sentence/switch
id: 381
r#  65206  1227  4


--- Sentence//end
id: 381
r#  65300  1228  39
p: .
t. 
|   If the current symbol is a function-symbol, identify
.  which procedure is named by the symbol


--- Sentence/switch
id: 382
r#  65301  1228  40


--- Sentence//end
id: 382
r#  65506  1232  14
p: .
t. 
|   If we allow
|  polymorphism  -  one name may reference multiple code-blocks,
|  distinguished by their signatures  -  then this process
|  requires examining the types of values further down the
.  program stack


--- Sentence/switch
id: 383
r#  65507  1232  15


--- Sentence//end
id: 383
r#  65755  1236  42
p: .
t. 
|   Once the correct procedure is identified,
|  transfer control to that function body (if compiled
|  correctly, the new callee will take its inputs off the
|  stack and push its own return values, which in turn
.  may serve as inputs to later subroutines)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 384
r#  65756  1236  43


--- Sentence//end
id: 384
r#  65915  1241  9
p: .
t. 
|  
|  For sake of discussion, I will call this a push/transfer execution
|  model, given the two main operations (pushing a value, or transferring
.  control)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 385
r#  65916  1241  10


--- Sentence//end
id: 385
r#  66068  1243  48
p: .
t. 
|   While this account is too simplistic for practical computer
|  languages, \clrmrAlt{11}{69}{a}it serves
.  as a useful case-study for real-world compilers

--- Sentence/end 
id: 385
r#  66069  1243  49

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 68
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 69
r#  66071  1245  1


--- Sentence/start
id: 386
r#  66071  1245  1

--- Sentence//end
id: 386
r#  66233  1247  33
p: .
t. 
|  Among the added complications relevant to a real-world push/transfer
|  implementation are  -  first  -  resolving polymorphically
.  overloaded function symbols


--- Sentence/switch
id: 387
r#  66234  1247  34


--- Sentence//end
id: 387
r#  66339  1248  59
p: .
t. 
|   By stipulation, the interpreter or compiler
.  has access to a list mapping procedure names to signatures


--- Sentence/switch
id: 388
r#  66340  1248  60


--- Sentence//end
id: 388
r#  66459  1250  46
p: .
t. 
|  Selecting a correct overload is straightforward if there is one exact
.  signature-match given the current stack types


--- Sentence/switch
id: 389
r#  66460  1250  47


--- Sentence//end
id: 389
r#  66600  1252  38
p: .
t. 
|   However, most modern
|  programming environments allow implicit type-casts (e.g., any integer is also
.  a decimal, with fractional part zero)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 390
r#  66601  1252  39


--- Sentence//end
id: 390
r#  66786  1255  9
p: .
t. 
|   Moreover, modern languages commonly
|  adopt template-metaprogramming and/or class-inheritance systems wherein
|  a given variable-symbol could have different types in different
.  contexts


--- Sentence/switch
id: 391
r#  66787  1255  10


--- Sentence//end
id: 391
r#  66911  1256  68
p: .
t. 
|   In these systems, to compute which overload matches a
.  given sequence of parameter-values is considerably more complicated

--- Sentence/end 
id: 391
r#  66912  1256  69

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 69
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 70
r#  66914  1258  1


--- Sentence/start
id: 392
r#  66914  1258  1

--- Sentence//end
id: 392
r#  67241  1262  43
p: .
t. 
|  A second difficulty for push/transfer mechanics is deriving the correct
|  sequence for presenting symbols to the interpreter (in real life, said
|  interpreter is more like an abstract representation guiding the compilation
|  of high-level code, but we can still reason about source
.  code as if it were interpreted on-the-fly)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 393
r#  67242  1262  44


--- Sentence//end
id: 393
r#  67384  1264  39
p: .
t. 
|   Usually this sequence is
|  different from the order in which tokens appear in source code, so symbols
.  have to be reordered for push/transfer


--- Sentence/switch
id: 394
r#  67385  1264  40


--- Sentence//end
id: 394
r#  67617  1267  41
p: .
t. 
|   Compilers must also deduce which tokens
|  are associated with which function-symbols, a process that involves
|  both syntax (e.g., nested parentheses) and semantics (e.g., order of operands,
.  like multiplication preempting addition)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 395
r#  67618  1267  42


--- Sentence//end
id: 395
r#  67963  1271  83
p: .
t. 
|   Parsing high-level code is therefore a
|  matter of reducing source files (which have extra structures such as nested
|  expressions) to a simpler representation based on token-sequences
|  (details like nesting become flattened to a single lineup, which works
.  because subroutines enforce a contract about how the program stack should be used)

--- Sentence/end 
id: 395
r#  67964  1271  84

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 70
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 71
r#  67967  1273  1


--- Sentence/start
id: 396
r#  67967  1273  1

--- Sentence//end
id: 396
r#  68094  1274  53
p: .
t. 
|  Despite its simplistic provenance, the push/transfer execution model
.  has some interesting resonance with natural language


--- Sentence/switch
id: 397
r#  68095  1274  54


--- Sentence//end
id: 397
r#  68334  1278  18
p: .
t. 
|   For one
|  thing, the syntactic process just described  -  parsing more-complex
|  code down to symbols arranged in a sequence ordered for push and transfer
|  actions  -  has some schematic resemblance to the role of syntax
.  in human language


--- Sentence/switch
id: 398
r#  68335  1278  19


--- Sentence//end
id: 398
r#  68532  1281  20
p: .
t. 
|   Moreover, semantic complexities such as
|  function polymorphism recall phenomena like multiple word-senses,
|  where lexical units are, in effect, overloaded with divergent
.  meanings by context

--- Sentence/end 
id: 398
r#  68533  1281  21

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 71
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 72
r#  68537  1283  1


--- Sentence/start
id: 399
r#  68537  1283  1

--- Sentence//end
id: 399
r#  68818  1287  19
p: .
t. 
|  Both computer code and natural language are, by many lights,
|  better represented in terms of parse-graphs than trees
|  (particularly in so far as some relations, like anaphora in the latter context
|  and side-effect propagation in the former, cut across tree-like
.  directed branches)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 400
r#  68819  1287  20


--- Sentence//end
id: 400
r#  68932  1288  64
p: .
t. 
|   With proper edge-filtering, tree visiting
.  algorithms (e.g., push/transfer) can be adopted to parse graphs


--- Sentence/switch
id: 401
r#  68933  1288  65


--- Sentence//end
id: 401
r#  69065  1291  21
p: .
t. 
|  Type-checking may be described as a graph-traversal
|  strategy where the program stack holds types rather
.  than concrete values


--- Sentence/switch
id: 402
r#  69066  1291  22


--- Sentence//end
id: 402
r#  69363  1297  9
p: .
t. 
|   Assuming
|  polymorphism, \clrmAlt{12}{73}a type-checker
|  must confirm that, given any stack-state with a
|  procedure symbol on top, there is a unique implementation
|  whose parameter-types match those symbolized lower on the stack
|  (if so, its output type is then placed on the stack
.  instead)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 403
r#  69364  1297  10


--- Sentence//end
id: 403
r#  69830  1305  67
p: .
t. 
|   Both type-checking
|  and push/transfer may thereby be modeled
|  as versions of a generic graph-traversal pattern, whose
|  central operations are crossing to unvisited child nodes;
|  pushing leaf-nodes' values (or types, etc.) onto an
|  external stack; and performing some external operation
|  on non-leaf nodes once all descendent branches have been explored
|  (in effect, navigating graphs with a state
.  machine holding stack-memory and a trace of previously-seen nodes)

--- Sentence/end 
id: 403
r#  69831  1305  68

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 72
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 73
r#  69833  1307  1


--- Sentence/start
id: 404
r#  69833  1307  1

--- Sentence//end
id: 404
r#  70150  1312  39
p: .
t. 
|  In short, whenever a directed graph models a computational
|  process, we can extract a postordering of nodes
|  representing the proper sequence for a push/transfer traversal;
|  and we can type-check relationships between
|  child nodes and parent nodes, the latter being interpreted
.  as procedures with specific signatures


--- Sentence/switch
id: 405
r#  70151  1312  40


--- Sentence//end
id: 405
r#  70276  1314  52
p: .
t. 
|   I believe
|  both of these operations  -  postordering and type-checking
.   -  have approximate correlates in natural language


--- Sentence/switch
id: 406
r#  70277  1314  53


--- Sentence//end
id: 406
r#  70460  1318  47
p: .
t. 
|  The mapping of a general parse-graph onto
|  a postorder traversal sequence recalls
|  underlying machineries for conceptual synthesis,
.  conformant to a word-by-word staging order


--- Sentence/switch
id: 407
r#  70461  1318  48


--- Sentence//end
id: 407
r#  70743  1324  63
p: .
t. 
|  Intuitively, nouns are akin to
|  values pushed onto a conceptual stack,
|  whereas verbs, adjectives, and other modifiers
|  embody cognitive transforms applied to concepts
|  currently residing on this stack
.  (albeit which, in the present context, is mostly metaphorical)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 408
r#  70744  1324  64


--- Sentence//end
id: 408
r#  70907  1327  66
p: .
t. 
|  Given this conceptual background, I will adopt the
|  phrase staging order as a linguistic
.  counterpart to computational push/transfer order

--- Sentence/end 
id: 408
r#  70908  1327  67

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 73
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 74
r#  70911  1329  1


--- Sentence/start
id: 409
r#  70911  1329  1

--- Sentence//end
id: 409
r#  71157  1333  32
p: .
t. 
|  Type-checking and postordering are functionalizable
|  similarities between natural and programming languages
|   -  whatever the metaphysical differences between how
|  they are realized, the two domains have some
.  functional structures in common


--- Sentence/switch
id: 410
r#  71158  1333  33


--- Sentence//end
id: 410
r#  71292  1336  16
p: .
t. 
|   By analogy,
|  human and computer vision share capabilities
|  whose functional roles in larger processing modules
.  can be analyzed


--- Sentence/switch
id: 411
r#  71293  1336  17


--- Sentence//end
id: 411
r#  71571  1340  32
p: .
t. 
|   Image-analysis tactics such as texture
|  segmentation, occlusion compensation, and gradient-vector inference
|  have detailed mathematical formulae that could offer functional insights into
|  visual perception (even if computer implementations may
.  be very different from brains')


--- Sentence/switch
id: 412
r#  71572  1340  33


--- Sentence//end
id: 412
r#  71810  1344  27
p: .
t. 
|   Postordering and type-checking are
|  not at the same level of quantitative sophistication,
|  so we don't necessarily move a theoretical needle very far
|  by detecting structural analogies between graph-postorder
.  and cognitive staging


--- Sentence/switch
id: 413
r#  71811  1344  28


--- Sentence//end
id: 413
r#  71981  1347  49
p: .
t. 
|   Still,
|  I believe these analogies imply a precis of the
|  syntax/semantics interface that is more cognitively realistic
.  than 's appeal to commutative diagrams


--- Sentence/switch
id: 414
r#  71982  1347  50

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 74
y: N/A

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 74
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 75
r#  72209  1357  1


--- Sentence/start
id: 415
r#  72209  1357  1

--- Sentence//end
id: 415
r#  72294  1358  28
p: .
t. 
|   Compilers
|  are stateful processing/workflow \textcolor{white}{platforms,
|  more than term-reduce artefacts.}
|  More substantially, postordering is not just a heuristic
.  for revisiting parse graphs


--- Sentence/switch
id: 416
r#  72295  1358  29


--- Sentence//end
id: 416
r#  72403  1360  34
p: .
t. 
|   Type reduction and
|  conceptual-synthesis conventions act as constraints
.  on parse-graphs to begin with


--- Sentence/switch
id: 417
r#  72404  1360  35


--- Sentence//end
id: 417
r#  72466  1361  35
p: .
t. 
|   Most sentences give rise
.  to many minimally plausible graphs


--- Sentence/switch
id: 418
r#  72467  1361  36


--- Sentence//end
id: 418
r#  72658  1364  33
p: .
t. 
|   Even when excluding
|  unrelated contexts like the family name, for instance, the
|  word trump in a \clrmr{12}{76}playing-card context can serve as noun,
.  verb, adjective, and even adverb


--- Sentence/switch
id: 419
r#  72659  1364  34


--- Sentence//end
id: 419
r#  72770  1366  23
p: .
t. 
|   The distribution of a
|  single word-sense over multiple syntactic categories is more a
.  norm than an exception


--- Sentence/switch
id: 420
r#  72771  1366  24


--- Sentence//end
id: 420
r#  72910  1368  28
p: .
t. 
|   This fact in itself suggests a proliferation
|  of plausible parse-graphs, even before addressing polysemy and
.  phrase-boundary ambiguities


--- Sentence/switch
id: 421
r#  72911  1368  29


--- Sentence//end
id: 421
r#  73110  1371  29
p: .
t. 
|   From many plausible candidates,
|  the set of correct parse graphs are those that meet
|  case-by-case criteria of coherence, some of which can be modeled in terms
.  of graph-traversal protocols

--- Sentence/end 
id: 421
r#  73111  1371  30

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 75
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 76
r#  73115  1373  1


--- Sentence/start
id: 422
r#  73115  1373  1

--- Sentence//end
id: 422
r#  73250  1374  67
p: .
t. 
|  Such processes are apparently interdependent, in that (for example)
.  part-of-speech assignment influences phrase nesting and vice-versa


--- Sentence/switch
id: 423
r#  73251  1374  68


--- Sentence//end
id: 423
r#  73403  1377  33
p: .
t. 
|  Language would be more computationally tractable if processing algorithms
|  had a logical order, with earlier ones'
.  results available to those later


--- Sentence/switch
id: 424
r#  73404  1377  34


--- Sentence//end
id: 424
r#  73725  1383  15
p: .
t. 
|   In reality,
|  it seems as if our minds subconsciously test out many
|  initial-condition sets for traversals (e.g., multiple ways
|  of construing syntactic categories) and converge on best solutions
|  (e.g., parts of speech and phrase boundaries
|  being mutually consistent, without deriving the former first,
.  or vice-versa)


--- Sentence/switch
id: 425
r#  73726  1383  16


--- Sentence//end
id: 425
r#  74093  1387  81
p: .
t. 
|   Conceptual synthesis and staging are
|  surely part of this process insofar as assessments of meaningfulness, on the
|  one hand, and well-formedness (on syntactic, semantic, and pragmatic criteria)
|  on the other, coexist in a kind of feedback loop, converging on the most plausible
.  construals in all domains (syntax, semantics, pragmatics, and conceptualization)

--- Sentence/end 
id: 425
r#  74094  1387  82

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 76
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 77
r#  74101  1389  1


--- Sentence/start
id: 426
r#  74101  1389  1

--- Sentence//end
id: 426
r#  74448  1395  47
p: .
t. 
|  This interpretation of parse-graphs  -  as
|  intermediate representation for sentence content,
|  endowed with post\-order and type-checking traversals
|    -  works by analogy to
|  (but also in place of) set-theoretic or (say) modal-logic
|  approaches, where languages are treated as a surface-level
.  manifestation of logico-mathematical rule sets


--- Sentence/switch
id: 427
r#  74449  1395  48


--- Sentence//end
id: 427
r#  74683  1399  31
p: .
t. 
|   A key difference,
|  however  -  apropos computer-code as metaphor for human
|  language  -  is that within transfer of control the space
|  of available functions is not restricted to those explicitly
.  implemented in high-level code


--- Sentence/switch
id: 428
r#  74684  1399  32


--- Sentence//end
id: 428
r#  75096  1406  46
p: .
t. 
|   Operating-system functions,
|  language runtimes, standard libraries, and other
|  pre-compiled procedures would be analogous to extralinguistic
|  cognitive competence that may be solicited by constructions
|  in discourse: conceptual transformations' staging-order position
|  might be established by syntactic and
|  semantic means, but their cognitive effects need not be
.  modeled solely in semantic or pragmatic terms

--- Sentence/end 
id: 428
r#  75097  1406  47

--- Paragraph/end 
id: 77
y: N/A


--- Paragraph/start
id: 78
r#  75102  1408  1


--- Sentence/start
id: 429
r#  75102  1408  1

